NAME
       gawk - pattern scanning and text processing language (GNU awk, also awk)

SYNOPSIS
       awk [-F SEP] [-v NAME=VALUE] 'PROGRAM' [FILE...]
       awk [-F SEP] [-v NAME=VALUE] -f PROGRAM-FILE [FILE...]

DESCRIPTION
       awk reads its input one line (record) at a time, splits each line into
       fields, and runs the PROGRAM on it. On RHEL, awk is GNU awk (gawk).

       A program is a list of pattern { action } pairs. For every line, each
       pattern is tested and, when it matches, its action runs. A missing
       pattern matches every line; a missing action prints the line.

           awk -F: '$3 >= 1000 { print $1 }' /etc/passwd

       prints the name (field 1) of every user whose UID (field 3) is 1000 or
       more.

   Fields and variables
       $0         the whole line
       $1, $2...  the fields; $NF is the last one
       NF         the number of fields in this line
       NR         the number of the line (counting all files)
       FNR        the number of the line in the current file
       FS         the input field separator (white space by default: runs
                  of spaces and tabs count as one)
       OFS        the separator print puts between items (a space)
       FILENAME   the current file

   Patterns
       /REGEX/              the line matches the regular expression
       $1 ~ /REGEX/         a field matches (!~ for does not match)
       $3 > 100, $1 == "x"  comparisons
       BEGIN, END           run before the first line, after the last
       PAT1 && PAT2, !PAT   and, not

   Actions
       print a, b           print the items with OFS between them
       printf "fmt", a, b   formatted print (%s, %d, %5.2f, \n)
       x = x + $2           variables need no declaration (start at 0 / "")
       count[$1]++          arrays indexed by text
       if (...) ... else ..., for (i=1; i<=NF; i++), for (k in arr), while
       next                 go on to the next line

   Functions
       length(s), substr(s, start, len), index(s, t), split(s, arr, sep),
       sub(/re/, "new"), gsub(/re/, "new"), match(s, /re/), tolower(s),
       toupper(s), sprintf(fmt, ...), int(x), system("command")

OPTIONS
       -F SEP Use SEP as the field separator, for example -F: or -F,.

       -v NAME=VALUE
              Set a variable before the program starts.

       -f FILE
              Read the program from FILE.

EXAMPLES
       Print the second column of the output of a command:

           df -h | awk '{ print $1, $5 }'

       Users with /bin/bash as their shell:

           awk -F: '$7 == "/bin/bash" { print $1 }' /etc/passwd

       Add up the sizes in the fifth column of ls -l:

           ls -l /etc | awk '{ sum += $5 } END { print sum }'

       Count lines per value of the first field of a CSV:

           awk -F, '{ n[$1]++ } END { for (k in n) print k, n[k] }' report.csv

       Use a shell variable in a program:

           awk -v min=1000 -F: '$3 >= min' /etc/passwd

SEE ALSO
       grep(1), sed(1), cut(1), sort(1)
